WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:20.200
<v Ira Glass>Ryan started going blind when he was 18, so it's been a long time now that he can't see. And one night he flies to Chicago for this work thing. And gets to his hotel room and he wants to call his wife back home in Canada to let her know that he's arrived safely. So all he needs to do is find the phone.
<v Ira Glass>瑞安在18岁时开始失明，所以他已经很长一段时间看不见了。一天晚上，他为了工作飞往芝加哥。到了酒店房间后，他想给远在加拿大的妻子打个电话，告诉她自己平安到达。所以他只需要找到电话就行了。

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:36.850
<v Ryan Knighton>And so I walk into the room and I find the bed. And then to the left of the bed, I feel along and I find this nightstand, which is where I expect the phone to be. And so I feel up the nightstand and there's no phone. Fine.
<v Ryan Knighton>我走进房间，找到了床。然后摸向床的左侧，找到了我以为放电话的床头柜。我摸了摸床头柜，上面没有电话。好吧。

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:43.940
<v Ryan Knighton>So I reach across the bed to the other side and find the other nightstand. And I feel that one up, and there's no phone.
<v Ryan Knighton>于是我伸长手臂越过床，摸向另一侧，找到了另一个床头柜。我又摸了摸那个，也没有电话。

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.380
<v Ira Glass>That's unusual, right?
<v Ira Glass>这很不寻常，对吧？

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.580
<v Ryan Knighton>It's a bit odd, right?
<v Ryan Knighton>有点奇怪，对吧？

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:58.090
<v Ira Glass>So Ryan can shuffle cautiously around till his knees graze into things. And that's how he finds a sofa. Which orients him.
<v Ira Glass>瑞安可以小心翼翼地挪动，直到膝盖碰到东西。他就是这样找到了沙发。这帮他确定了方位。

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:08.090
<v Ryan Knighton>And so I turn to where I think there might be a table, and poof! There's a coffee table. So I grope this coffee table for a while and there's no phone on it.
<v Ryan Knighton>于是我转向我以为有桌子的地方，砰！有个咖啡桌。我摸索了半天，上面也没电话。

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:11.160
<v Ira Glass>Grope is kind of a funny word to use for this.
<v Ira Glass>用“摸索”这个词来形容这件事挺有意思的。

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:18.200
<v Ryan Knighton>It sort of feels that way, though. You're just sort of-- because you don't know where anything begins or ends, so you really maul it.
<v Ryan Knighton>不过感觉确实如此。你只是……因为你不知道东西从哪里开始又在哪里结束，所以你真的是在乱摸。

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:37.770
<v Ira Glass>He says that as he moves around any new place, he doesn't exactly draw a map in his head. He says that it's more like wandering around in a first-person video game. One where nothing is visible until he touches it. So he figures OK, let's see what is on the other side of this coffee table that he's found. And he edges forward in the room.
<v Ira Glass>他说，当他在新地方走动时，他并不会在脑海里画地图。他说这更像是在玩第一人称电子游戏。一个除非摸到否则什么都看不见的游戏。所以他想，好吧，看看他找到的这个咖啡桌后面有什么。于是他在房间里向前挪动。

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:55.500
<v Ryan Knighton>And I find there's a desk. And I'm like, aha, the desk! So I feel around on the desk and there's a lamp and there's the notepad I'll never use, and there's stuff, but there's no phone.
<v Ryan Knighton>我发现了一张书桌。我心想，啊哈，书桌！于是我在书桌上摸了摸，上面有盏台灯，还有我永远不会用的记事本，还有别的东西，但就是没有电话。

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:07.870
<v Ryan Knighton>So I'm left to my last blind guy resort, which is I go back to the beginning. Back to the bed and I find the wall. And I start Marcel Marceauing the walls. I'm wiping them up and down.
<v Ryan Knighton>所以我只能使出盲人的最后一招，那就是回到起点。回到床边，找到墙。我开始像马塞尔·马索那样顺着墙摸。我上下摸索着墙壁。

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:20.730
<v Ryan Knighton>And I round the fourth corner and I get to the bathroom, and I go past the bathroom and there's nothing. And I feel behind me again and the bed is back behind me again. So I've circled this room.
<v Ryan Knighton>我转过第四个角落，到了浴室，我走过浴室，什么都没有。我又向身后摸了摸，床又在后面了。所以我把这个房间绕了一圈。

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:30.150
<v Ryan Knighton>And I even thought, well maybe it's a super fancy hotel. And maybe there's a phone in the bathroom. And I go in there and there's nothing.
<v Ryan Knighton>我甚至想，也许这是家超豪华酒店。也许浴室里有电话。我走进去，结果什么都没有。

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:47.800
<v Ryan Knighton>So I circle the room two more times this way, wiping it down. And I check the coffee table again. I check the desk again. And I just figure, forget it. I'll just go to bed and try again tomorrow.
<v Ryan Knighton>所以我用这种方法又绕了两圈，把墙都摸了一遍。我又检查了一次咖啡桌。我又检查了一次书桌。我就想，算了。我还是睡觉吧，明天再试。

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.950
<v Ira Glass>So he goes to bed. Doesn't call his wife. Sleeps.
<v Ira Glass>于是他上床睡觉了。没给妻子打电话。睡着了。

00:02:52.950 --> 00:03:07.240
<v Ira Glass>And in the morning, he wakes up to the sound of something curious. A phone ringing. And groggy, he follows the sound, and finds somehow now, there is a phone in this room.
<v Ira Glass>第二天早上，他被一种奇怪的声音吵醒了。电话铃声。他迷迷糊糊地顺着声音走去，发现房间里居然真的有一部电话了。

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:18.460
<v Ryan Knighton>And the phone is on a coffee table. Now I know I felt that thing up to an illicit degree. I mauled that coffee table, and there was nothing on that table last night.
<v Ryan Knighton>电话就在咖啡桌上。我明明记得昨晚把那东西摸得一清二楚。我把那张咖啡桌摸了个遍，昨晚上面什么都没有。

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:26.320
<v Ryan Knighton>And so I answer the phone and it's my wife. And she says, "why didn't you call me last night?" And I said, "well there was no phone. But there is now."
<v Ryan Knighton>于是我接起电话，是我妻子。她说：“你昨晚为什么不给我打电话？”我说：“好吧，昨晚没电话。但现在有了。”

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.170
<v Ira Glass>She doesn't believe him that there was no phone, but this is kind of par for the course when you're married to a blind guy.
<v Ira Glass>她不相信昨晚没有电话，但当你和一个盲人结婚时，这种情况见怪不怪了。

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:40.720
<v Ryan Knighton>And so we talk. And then I hang up the phone and I go to get back into bed, and there's now a wall there.
<v Ryan Knighton>我们聊了一会儿。挂断电话后，我准备回床上睡觉，结果那里现在多了一堵墙。

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:48.530
<v Ira Glass>A wall. Where the bed should be is now a wall. He feels for the sofa. The sofa's right where it should be.
<v Ira Glass>一堵墙。本该是床的地方现在成了一堵墙。他摸了摸沙发。沙发还在它该在的位置。

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:59.040
<v Ira Glass>The wall behind the sofa is right where it should be, right there in place. He feels along the sofa again, inches towards where the bed should be, and yes, it's still a wall.
<v Ira Glass>沙发后面的墙也在它该在的位置。他又顺着沙发摸过去，向本该是床的地方挪动，没错，还是堵墙。

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:08.280
<v Ryan Knighton>And I'm totally disoriented at this point. Like it's funny and it's also sort of terrifying. Because I know the bed was there, and now there's a wall.
<v Ryan Knighton>我当时完全糊涂了。这既好笑又有点恐怖。因为我知道床就在那儿，可现在变成了一堵墙。

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:22.940
<v Ryan Knighton>And I keep touching the wall, thinking maybe this time it'll go away. And I go to the left, and there's another wall now. And I'm a grown man and I'm lost in a hotel room.
<v Ryan Knighton>我不停地摸那堵墙，想着也许这一次它会消失。我向左走，现在又出现了另一堵墙。我已经是个成年人了，却在酒店房间里迷了路。

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.290
<v Ira Glass>So what's your next move? What do you do?
<v Ira Glass>那你下一步怎么做？你打算怎么办？

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:36.050
<v Ryan Knighton>I ended up doing the Marcel Marceau thing. I start wiping the walls, feeling my way along the edges, and it wraps back around till I find the bed is actually behind me.
<v Ryan Knighton>我最后又用了马塞尔·马索那一招。我开始顺着墙摸，摸着边缘走，最后又绕了回来，发现床其实就在我身后。

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:44.700
<v Ira Glass>He was in a part of the room that he hadn't encountered the night before. This was an alcove on a side of the bed that he just never discovered.
<v Ira Glass>他当时身处房间里他前一天晚上没接触过的部分。这是一个他从未发现的床侧凹室。

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:53.240
<v Ryan Knighton>So here's what the room actually looked like. There are two coffee tables and two sofas on the left and the right side of the bed.
<v Ryan Knighton>实际上房间的样子是这样的。床的左右两侧各有一张咖啡桌和一张沙发。

00:04:53.240 --> 00:05:08.510
<v Ira Glass>The mistake that he made the night before was this. When he was Marcel Marceauing the walls, he got 3/4 of the way around the room and got to the last wall. And he didn't actually feel all the way along that wall until it met another wall.
<v Ira Glass>他前一天晚上犯的错误是这样的。当他像马塞尔·马索那样摸墙时，他绕了房间四分之三的路程，到了最后一堵墙。但他并没有沿着那堵墙一直摸到它与另一堵墙交汇。

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:17.390
<v Ira Glass>Basically, he went a little ways down that wall, felt that the bed was behind him. So when he realized that the bed was behind him, be figured he was done. He stopped feeling that wall.
<v Ira Glass>基本上，他顺着墙走了一段，感觉到床在身后。所以当他意识到床在身后时，他觉得任务完成了。他停止了摸索那面墙。

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:24.270
<v Ira Glass>He just assumed that the wall continued for another eight feet or so. But it didn't continue. It stopped. And there was this alcove.
<v Ira Glass>他只是想当然地认为那堵墙还延伸了大约八英尺。但它并没有延伸。它到头了。然后就是一个凹室。

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:35.230
<v Ryan Knighton>And this is the problem. When you're blind, you just can't assume anything. And the problem is you get a picture in your mind and if you get it wrong, you just live inside the mistake.
<v Ryan Knighton>这就是问题所在。当你失明时，你不能想当然。问题在于你脑海中形成了一幅画面，如果你弄错了，你就只能活在这个错误之中。

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:39.960
<v Ira Glass>This kind of thing happens to him a lot. Way, way more than you would think.
<v Ira Glass>这种事情在他身上经常发生。比你想象的要多得多。

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:51.800
<v Ira Glass>Two weeks before our interview, he got lost in another hotel room, this one in Los Angeles. He couldn't find the door to get out of the room. He says that during the decade that he slowly went blind--
<v Ira Glass>在我们采访的前两周，他在洛杉矶的另一家酒店房间里迷路了。他找不到离开房间的门。他说在他缓慢失明的那十年里——

00:05:51.800 --> 00:06:04.400
<v Ryan Knighton>It took me a long time to come to understand that blindness actually wasn't the main problem. The main problem was embarrassment. That I had to give myself over to the slapstick of things.
<v Ryan Knighton>我花了很长时间才明白，失明其实不是主要问题。主要问题是尴尬。我不得不屈服于那些笨拙滑稽的事情。

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:20.050
<v Ira Glass>To state the obvious, sometimes it is just a lot easier to see things. Clears a lot of things up. And today on our radio show, we have all kinds of stories of people trying to take things that are normally invisible to them and make them visible.
<v Ira Glass>显而易见，有时能看见东西确实容易多了。能让很多事情变得清晰。今天在我们的广播节目中，我们有各种关于人们努力将通常对自己不可见的事物变为可见的故事。

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:46.590
<v Ira Glass>I'm talking about unspoken feelings. I'm talking about people's secret lives. I'm talking, in a very literal way, about me and the other people doing stories on today's radio program. As people on the radio, usually we are invisible, but today we are bringing you excerpts from the show that we did on stage in front of people last week in New York City, and then beamed into movie theaters all across the United States and Canada and Australia.
<v Ira Glass>我说的是无法言说的心情。我说的是人们的秘密生活。从字面意义上讲，我说的是我和其他在今天的广播节目中讲述故事的人。作为广播从业者，我们通常是不可见的，但今天我们要为你播放我们上周在纽约市观众面前表演的节目片段，然后通过卫星信号转播到美国、加拿大和澳大利亚各地的电影院。

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:59.980
<v Ira Glass>Some of the stuff on that show-- in fact, a lot of the stuff on that show-- was way too visual to put on the radio. But the rest of the show consisted of these great stories from David Sedaris and David Rakoff, and Tig Notaro, and others. We have a really nice show for you today.
<v Ira Glass>节目中的一些内容——事实上，节目中很大一部分内容——太视觉化了，不适合放在广播里。但节目的其余部分由大卫·塞达里斯、大卫·拉科夫、蒂格·诺塔洛等人的精彩故事组成。我们今天为您准备了一个非常棒的节目。

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:13.670
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:37.660
<v Ira Glass>Act One: Does a bear hit in the woods? So let's go to the first story that I'm gonna play you from the cinema event that we did. The guy who you just heard, actually, Ryan Knighton. He has this story that is not about what is invisible to him, as somebody who can't see. It's about being invisible. Here he is, Ryan Knighton.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：熊在树林里会排泄吗？那么让我们开始播放我们举办的电影院活动中的第一个故事。你刚才听到的那个人，瑞安·奈顿。他有一个故事，不是关于作为一个看不见的人对他来说什么是不可见的。而是关于被隐形。他就是瑞安·奈顿。

00:07:37.660 --> 00:08:03.930
<v Ryan Knighton>I couldn't wait to tell my daughter that I'm a blind guy. And I'm not saying I was excited to tell her, I'm saying I couldn't wait to tell her in a way that she could actually grasp the basic concept of blindness. The trouble is, a two-year-old can't imagine what it's like to be another person, let alone imagine an entirely different physical reality like blindness.
<v Ryan Knighton>我迫不及待地想告诉女儿我是一个盲人。我不是说我兴奋地告诉她，我是说我迫不及待地想以一种她能真正理解失明这个基本概念的方式告诉她。问题是，一个两岁的孩子无法想象成为另一个人是什么感觉，更不用说想象失明这样一种完全不同的身体现实了。

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:24.730
<v Ryan Knighton>I'd say to her, "Papa sees what you see when you close your eyes, but mine are open." Which makes no sense. To anybody. So the miscommunications began to pile up between us.
<v Ryan Knighton>我会对她说：“爸爸看到的是你闭上眼睛时看到的东西，但我的眼睛是睁着的。”这毫无意义。对任何人来说都是。所以我们之间的沟通误解开始堆积。

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:44.220
<v Ryan Knighton>One day I'm standing in the hallway of our house and Tess either kicked or rolled this foam soccer ball to me. Foam soccer balls are really quiet. You see my problem.
<v Ryan Knighton>有一天我站在家里的走廊上，泰丝把这个泡沫足球踢给我或者滚给我。泡沫足球真的很安静。你明白我的问题了吧。

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:57.410
<v Ryan Knighton>And it rolled by, yes, and I ignored it. But I didn't know it was there. And she got upset. She wants to know why didn't I kick it back to her? Why didn't I want to play with her? And she began to cry.
<v Ryan Knighton>它确实滚过去了，我没理会。但我不知道它在那里。她很难过。她想知道为什么我不把它踢回去？为什么我不想和她玩？她开始哭。

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:07.710
<v Ryan Knighton>Now I don't know what's going on at all. So I'm just saying things like, "what's wrong, punkin?" And like, "hey, why don't you go get your ball or something?"
<v Ryan Knighton>我完全不知道发生了什么。所以我只是说些类似“怎么了，宝贝？”还有“嘿，你为什么不去把球拿回来之类的？”

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:28.460
<v Ryan Knighton>Yeah, my wife did see this happen later on. And only then did I learn how I was rejecting my kid all day. And part of me felt useless as a father. And another part of me just felt really angry at NERF.
<v Ryan Knighton>是的，我妻子后来确实看到了这一幕。直到那时，我才知道自己整天都在拒绝我的孩子。我的一部分觉得自己作为一个父亲毫无用处。另一部分则对NERF（泡沫玩具厂商）感到非常生气。

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:49.120
<v Ryan Knighton>Another time I picked up Tess at a daycare. And "Papa, Papa!" she screams as I walk in. And she sees me, and I squat down and I open my arms, and I wait for the hug. Because it's best that I wait, because the floor is dotted with babies between us. And nothing ruins our sweet moment like me stomping on babies.
<v Ryan Knighton>还有一次我去托儿所接泰丝。我走进去时她尖叫着“爸爸，爸爸！”她看见我了，我蹲下来张开双臂，等待拥抱。因为最好还是等她过来，因为我们之间到处都是婴儿。没有什么比我踩到婴儿更能破坏我们甜蜜的时刻了。

00:09:49.120 --> 00:10:05.320
<v Ryan Knighton>So her body slams into mine and she wraps herself in a monkey hug, and I tell her how much I've missed her. And of course, to that, she cries. But she cried on the other side of the room.
<v Ryan Knighton>于是她的身体撞进我怀里，紧紧抱着我，我告诉她我有多想她。当然，听到这儿她哭了。但她是在房间的另一头哭。

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:29.430
<v Ryan Knighton>And suddenly, I feel this body and it's not familiar. And in fact, it's a little boy. And Tess is crying "Papa, Papa!" as if the word itself hurts, on the other side of the room. And so she just can't understand why I've hugged another child And I chase after the sound of her, and I'm sorry about the babies.
<v Ryan Knighton>我突然感觉到一个身体，但很陌生。实际上，是一个小男孩。而泰丝在房间另一头哭喊着“爸爸，爸爸！”，仿佛这个词本身就很伤人。她无法理解为什么我拥抱了另一个孩子。我追着她的声音跑去，我很抱歉撞到了婴儿们。

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:50.380
<v Ryan Knighton>So the miscommunications piled up, and they were mostly just little heartbreaks, but sometimes they were dangerous. There's times when I would walk her to the daycare in the morning. And I work at a university, and we'd walk across campus together in the morning. And she'd be in one of those NASCAR roll cage backpack things, which are great for blind fathers.
<v Ryan Knighton>所以沟通误解越积越多，它们大多只是小小的伤心事，但有时却很危险。有时早上我会陪她去托儿所。我在大学工作，我们早上会一起穿过校园。她会坐在那种NASCAR防滚架式的背包里，这对盲人父亲来说很棒。

00:10:50.380 --> 00:11:11.250
<v Ryan Knighton>And we'd make our way across campus, and it's beautiful. It's in the mountains and it's forested. And I say this just so you can understand my legitimate panic when from her backpack she said to me one of her few words. She said "bear."
<v Ryan Knighton>我们穿过校园，那里很美。它坐落在森林茂密的山区。我这么说只是为了让你理解我当时合法的恐慌，当她从背包里对我说出她为数不多的词汇之一时。她说“熊”。

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.190
<v Ryan Knighton>And I froze.
<v Ryan Knighton>我僵住了。

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.750
<v Ryan Knighton>"What's that, punkin?"
<v Ryan Knighton>“那是什么，宝贝？”

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:21.700
<v Ryan Knighton>And I turned, 'cause I can still see some smears, so I looked for a big black one.
<v Ryan Knighton>我转过身，因为我还能看到一些模糊的色块，所以我寻找一个巨大的黑色东西。

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:22.830
<v Ryan Knighton>"Bear."
<v Ryan Knighton>“熊。”

