<v Ira Glass>Taylor Dayne, in a sequined mini-dress, is singing to Tig. Tig is sitting on this stool onstage, her arms are crossed, she's looking sort of skeptical. And Taylor's trying to win her over.
<v Ira Glass>身穿亮片迷你裙的泰勒·戴恩正在为蒂格唱歌。蒂格坐在台上的高脚凳上，双臂交叉，表情看起来有点怀疑。泰勒正在努力赢得她的好感。

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:52.620
<v Taylor Dayne>You know the words. I see you in diners. In coffee beans. My speaking voice.
<v Taylor Dayne>你知道歌词的。我在餐馆里见到你。在咖啡豆里。我的说话声。

00:28:52.620 --> 00:29:04.570
<v Ira Glass>So it's around here in the song that Taylor does start to win Tig over.
<v Ira Glass>所以在歌曲的这个阶段，泰勒确实开始赢得蒂格的好感了。

00:29:04.570 --> 00:29:23.070
<v Taylor Dayne>She's with me now. Show 'em what you got, Tig. Let's go.
<v Taylor Dayne>她现在跟我在一起了。秀出你的本领，蒂格。来吧。

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:27.860
<v Ira Glass>So Tig does some dance moves.
<v Ira Glass>于是蒂格做了一些舞蹈动作。

00:29:27.860 --> 00:29:29.290
<v Taylor Dayne>Give it to 'em good.
<v Taylor Dayne>好好给他们露一手。

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:42.150
<v Ira Glass>They're laughing 'cause she busts out vintage Michael Jackson moves.
<v Ira Glass>他们在大笑，因为她跳出了复古的迈克尔·杰克逊舞步。

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:43.620
<v Taylor Dayne>You like that singing voice?
<v Taylor Dayne>你喜欢那个唱歌的声音吗？

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.090
<v Tig Notaro>I love it.
<v Tig Notaro>我爱它。

00:29:45.090 --> 00:30:25.240
<v Taylor Dayne>I love you love my voice! Tig!
<v Taylor Dayne>我爱你喜欢我的声音！蒂格！

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:30.410
<v Tig Notaro>Taylor! I love your voice.
<v Tig Notaro>泰勒！我爱你的声音。

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:32.320
<v Ira Glass>Taylor Dayne!
<v Ira Glass>泰勒·戴恩！

00:30:32.320 --> 00:30:33.310
<v Taylor Dayne>Thank you!
<v Taylor Dayne>谢谢！

00:30:33.310 --> 00:30:41.250
<v Ira Glass>Taylor Dayne!
<v Ira Glass>泰勒·戴恩！

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:59.130
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, David Rakoff's seven step process for grating cheese, David Sedaris, and other highlights from the show that we did on stage and beamed into movie theaters last week. In a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，大卫·拉科夫的刨奶酪七步法，大卫·塞达里斯，以及我们上周在舞台上表演并转播到电影院的其他亮点。一会儿回来，芝加哥公共广播和国际公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:15.200
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today on our show, we're bringing you stories from the episode that we did on stage in New York at the Skirball Center for the Performing Arts last week, and beamed into movie theaters all over North America.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目当然都会选择一个主题，带给您关于该主题的各种故事。今天在我们的节目中，我们要为您带来上周在纽约斯基尔鲍尔表演艺术中心舞台上表演、并转播到北美各地电影院的一期节目。

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:26.960
<v Ira Glass>As I said earlier, about half this show is just way too visual to ever put on the radio. It had dance and animation and all kinds of stuff. But half was a regular show like we always do. The theme was "The Invisible Made Visible."
<v Ira Glass>正如我之前所说，这个节目大约有一半的内容太视觉化了，无法放在广播里。它有舞蹈、动画和各种东西。但有一半是像我们平时那样常规的节目。主题是“让隐形变得可见”。

00:31:26.960 --> 00:31:46.940
<v Ira Glass>One of the things that we have put up online that you can actually see right now for free, is a short film made by Mike Birbiglia for this show. It co-stars Terry Gross from NPR's Fresh Air, and it totally got huge laughs, totally killed in theaters. It is about what happens that you do not see when you listen to Fresh Air.
<v Ira Glass>我们在网上发布的东西中，你可以现在免费看到的一项，是迈克·比比利亚为这个节目拍摄的一部短片。它由NPR《Fresh Air》节目的泰瑞·格罗斯联合主演，在电影院里引起了巨大的笑声，完全火了。它是关于当你听《Fresh Air》时，你看不到会发生什么。

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:52.640
<v Ira Glass>You can find that on YouTube, just search there. Or go to our website, thisamericanlife.org, for the link.
<v Ira Glass>你可以在YouTube上找到它，只要搜索一下。或者去我们的网站thisamericanlife.org获取链接。

00:31:52.640 --> 00:31:58.680
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three: Stiff as a board, light as a feather.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第三幕。第三幕：硬如木板，轻如羽毛。

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:27.350
<v Ira Glass>In our bodies, blood moves, cells appear and cells die off, proteins form and are consumed, all invisibly to us. Until the moment that something goes wrong. And then we have no choice but to see the effects. The next story, from our live show, is from David Rakoff.
<v Ira Glass>在我们的身体里，血液流动，细胞出现又消失，蛋白质形成又被消耗，这一切对我们来说都是隐形的。直到发生问题的时刻。然后我们就别无选择，只能面对后果。下一个故事，来自我们的现场秀，是大卫·拉科夫的。

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:43.900
<v David Rakoff>It hardly merits the term dream, it's such a throwaway moment. But I've had it three times now. The dream, or dreamlet, goes like this. I say to an unidentified companion, "Hey, watch this."
<v David Rakoff>这几乎称不上梦，只是一个微不足道的瞬间。但我已经做了三次了。这个梦，或者说小梦，是这样的。我对一个不知名的同伴说：“嘿，看这个。”

00:32:43.900 --> 00:33:22.580
<v David Rakoff>It's the punchline to that old joke, "what are an idiot's last words?" Except in my case, it is already too late. The idiot has already acted upon his idiot brag, the shallow part of the quarry has been dived into, the electric fence down by the rail yards unsuccessfully scaled, and my Trans Am has already failed to make it around Dead Man's Curve or down Killers Hill or off of, I don't know, Prom Night Suicide Cliff.
<v David Rakoff>这是那个老笑话“白痴的临终遗言是什么？”的梗。但在我这儿，已经太晚了。那个白痴已经对自己愚蠢的炫耀付诸行动了，采石场浅水区已经跳了，通往铁路货场的电栅栏已经尝试攀爬过却失败了，而我的庞蒂亚克火鸟跑车已经没能绕过“死者弯道”或冲下“杀手坡”，或者坠下什么“舞会自杀崖”。

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:53.490
<v David Rakoff>I had surgery last December-- my fourth in as many years-- to remove a tenacious and nasty tumor behind my left collarbone. I've also had radiation and about a year and a half's worth of chemo and counting. This last operation severed the nerves of my left arm, which relieved me of a great deal of pain. I'd spent three years prior to that popping enough Oxycontin to satisfy every man, woman, and child in Wasilla.
<v David Rakoff>我去年十二月做了手术——这是四年来的第四次——切除左锁骨后方一个顽固而恶心的肿瘤。我还接受了放射治疗和一年半的化疗，而且还在继续。这次手术切断了我左臂的神经，减轻了我很多痛苦。在那之前的三年里，我服用的奥昔康定足以满足瓦西拉的每一个男人、女人和孩子。

00:33:53.490 --> 00:34:16.010
<v David Rakoff>But the surgery also left me with what's known as a flail limb. It is attached, but aside from being able to shrug Talmudically, I can neither move nor feel my left arm. It now hangs from my side heavy and insensate as a bag of oranges.
<v David Rakoff>但这次手术也给我留下了一个所谓的“连枷肢”。它连在身上，但除了能像塔木德学者那样耸肩之外，我的左臂既不能动也没有感觉。它现在挂在我的身侧，像一袋橙子一样沉重而麻木。

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:37.740
<v David Rakoff>But this is a dream after all. So "hey, watch this," I say. And up goes the left arm. The resurrection of the dead limb feels both utterly logical and completely magical. But it is precisely that magical feeling that lets me know immediately that I have moved in error, and the jig, as it always is, is soon to be up.
<v David Rakoff>但毕竟这是个梦。所以我说“嘿，看这个”。然后左臂抬了起来。枯萎肢体的复活感觉既完全合乎逻辑又无比神奇。但正是那种神奇的感觉让我立即知道我犯了个错，而骗局，像往常一样，很快就会被揭穿。

00:34:37.740 --> 00:34:51.840
<v David Rakoff>I either literally pinch myself or snap my fingers in my ears trying to establish some reality. Or I ask someone, "is this real?" But I already know.
<v David Rakoff>我要么真的掐自己，要么在耳边打响指，试图建立某种现实。或者我问别人，“这是真的吗？”但我已经知道了。

00:34:51.840 --> 00:35:13.360
<v David Rakoff>There are some questions in life, the very speaking of which are their own undoing. "Am I fired? Is this a date? Are you breaking up with me?" "Yes. No. Yes."
<v David Rakoff>生活中有些问题，一说出口本身就是一种毁灭。“我被解雇了吗？这是约会吗？你要和我分手吗？”“是的。不。是的。”

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:30.560
<v David Rakoff>The voice, my voice, that is asking "is this real" is the sound that is waking me up to the world where-- alas-- the dream's a total cliche. Anyone with one working limb would dream it, which frankly, yawn.
<v David Rakoff>那个问“这是真的吗”的声音，我自己的声音，是把我唤醒到这个世界的声音——唉——梦境纯属老套。任何只有一个可用肢体的人都会做这个梦，老实说，真是无聊透顶。

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:56.080
<v David Rakoff>The one difference I might point to is how I move in the dream. The limb floats up like a table at a seance. I am one of those empty windsock men outside of used car lots who suddenly billows up into three dimensional life. The arm rises and there at the top of my gesture, my fingers frill like a sea anemone caught by an unseen current.
<v David Rakoff>我能指出的唯一区别是我在梦中如何移动。肢体像降神会上的桌子一样浮起来。我是那种二手车行外突然膨胀成三维生命的充气木偶人之一。手臂升起，在动作的最高处，我的手指像被某种看不见的洋流捕获的海葵一样颤动。

00:35:56.080 --> 00:36:25.860
<v David Rakoff>There is no functionality to it. I am not reaching for something, pulling the pin from a fire extinguisher, or hailing a cab. Mine is an extremely graceful and, I'll just say it, faggy gesture. Unmistakably, a gesture from ballet class, a gesture of someone who danced. Which is very different from having been a dancer.
<v David Rakoff>它没有功能。我不是在拿东西，拔掉灭火器的插销，或者招手拦出租车。我的动作极其优雅，我就直说了，很“娘”。毫无疑问，这是舞蹈课上的姿势，是一个跳过舞的人的姿势。这和“曾经是一名舞者”有很大的不同。

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:47.240
<v David Rakoff>I danced a lot, all through my childhood bedroom. It's an incredibly generic trait for a certain type of boy. Like a straight boy being obsessed with baseball, except it's better.
<v David Rakoff>我童年时在卧室里跳过很多舞。这是某种男孩极其普通的特质。就像直男痴迷于棒球，只不过跳舞更好。

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:56.090
<v David Rakoff>And after that, I danced fairly serious in university. But I was never really that great. And it's close to three decades ago now.
<v David Rakoff>在那之后，我在大学里比较认真地跳过。但我从来没有那么出色。而且那已经是近三十年前的事了。

00:36:56.090 --> 00:37:22.420
<v David Rakoff>I took classes across the street at the women's college, not the most rigorous of places. And as a boy, one of at most any three males in any of the classes, the standards were even laxer. Any illusions I might have had about my scant abilities were blown to smithereens by the occasional class I took at a proper dance studio down on 55th Street in the real world, where actual New York City dancers came.
<v David Rakoff>我在街对面的女子学院上课，那并不是什么严格的地方。作为一个男孩，在任何班级里最多三个男生之一，标准就更松了。关于我那点微薄能力的任何幻想，都被我在第55街真实世界的一家正规舞蹈工作室偶尔上的课给彻底粉碎了，那里有真正的纽约市舞者。

00:37:22.420 --> 00:38:03.780
<v David Rakoff>It was an exercise in humiliation and trying to make myself as invisible as possible. The only saving grace, indeed, the only reason I really went at all, were the 20 minutes in the men's changing room before and after. There's almost no way to explain it to a younger person, but you cannot imagine the rare thrill it was to see beautiful naked people in those pre-internet days of the early 1980s. I would walk slowly to the subway undone, clinging to the sides of buildings like someone who'd just come from the eye doctor.
<v David Rakoff>那是一场屈辱的练习，我试图让自己尽可能隐形。唯一的安慰，实际上，我真正去的唯一原因，是课前和课后在男更衣室里的那20分钟。对年轻人几乎无法解释，但你无法想象在1980年代初那前互联网时代，看到美丽的裸体是多么难得的刺激。我会在出门时慢慢走到地铁站，像刚从眼科医生那儿出来一样紧贴着建筑物边缘。

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:30.130
<v David Rakoff>If I retained anything from dancing, it's a physical precision that certainly helps in my new daily one-armed tasks. They're the same as my old two-armed chores. They're not epic or horrifying. Some of them don't even take much longer, but they're all to one degree or another, more annoying than they used to be, requiring planning, strategy, and a certain enhanced gracefulness.
<v David Rakoff>如果说我从舞蹈中学到了什么，那就是一种身体的精确感，这无疑有助于我现在的单臂日常任务。它们和我的双臂家务一样。并不史诗也不恐怖。有些甚至不需要多花时间，但它们或多或少都比以前更烦人，需要规划、策略和某种增强的优雅感。

00:38:30.130 --> 00:38:56.090
<v David Rakoff>Oral hygiene. Hold the handle of the toothbrush between your teeth the way FDR or Burgess Meredith playing The Penguin bit down on their cigarette holders. Put the toothpaste on the brush, recap the tube, put it away. You really have to keep things tidy, because if they pile up, you'll just be in the soup. Then reverse the brush and put the bristles in your mouth, proceed.
<v David Rakoff>口腔卫生。把牙刷柄含在牙齿之间，就像扮演企鹅人的富兰克林·罗斯福或伯吉斯·梅迪斯那样咬住他们的烟嘴。把牙膏挤在刷子上，盖好盖子，收好牙刷。你真的得保持整洁，因为如果堆积起来，你就会倒大霉。然后反转牙刷把刷毛放进嘴里，开始刷牙。

00:38:56.090 --> 00:39:11.100
<v David Rakoff>Washing your right arm. Soap up your right thigh in the shower, put your foot up on the edge of the tub, and then move your arm over your soapy lower limb back and forth like an old-timey barbershop razor strop.
<v David Rakoff>洗右臂。在淋浴时把右大腿打上肥皂，把脚抬到浴缸边缘，然后把手臂在肥皂包裹的下肢上来回移动，就像旧式理发店的剃刀磨刀皮带一样。

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:22.910
<v David Rakoff>Grating cheese. Get a pot with a looped handle, the heavier the better. This will anchor the bowl that you want the cheese to go into. Put the bowl into the pot.
<v David Rakoff>刨奶酪。拿一个带圆环手柄的锅，越重越好。这会固定住你想盛奶酪的碗。把碗放进锅里。

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:51.100
<v David Rakoff>Now take a wooden spoon and feed it through the handle of the grater and the loop of the pot, and then tuck the end down into the waistband of your jeans. Clean underpants are a good idea. Jam yourself up against the kitchen counter and go to town.
<v David Rakoff>现在拿一把木勺，把它穿过刨丝器的手柄和锅的圆环，然后把末端塞进你的牛仔裤腰带里。穿干净的内裤是个好主意。把自己挤在厨房台面边，大干一场。

00:39:51.100 --> 00:40:18.330
<v David Rakoff>Special kitchen note: always, always, always have your bum hand safely out of the way, preferably in a sling since you now have a limb that you could literally-- no joke-- cook on the stove without even knowing it. Which makes me feel not like a freak, exactly, but well actually, like a freak.
<v David Rakoff>厨房特别说明：一定要、一定要、一定要把你的废手安全地放好，最好用吊带托着，因为你现在有一条肢体可以在炉子上烤熟了却毫无知觉。这让我感觉不像怪胎，但也确实，像个怪胎。

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:29.890
<v David Rakoff>At dinner with friends recently, the conversation turned to what about yourself was still in need of change? They all seem to feel that they were living half-lives.
<v David Rakoff>最近和朋友聚餐时，话题转向了你身上还有什么需要改变的地方？他们似乎都觉得自己在过着半辈子。

00:40:29.890 --> 00:40:59.670
<v David Rakoff>One fellow hoped that he could be more like the god Pan, unabashedly lusty and embracing experience with gusto. Another wanted to feel less disengaged at key moments, able to feel more fully committedly human, and less like that old science fiction B movie trope: "What is this wetness on Triton 3000's face plate?" "Why, space robot, you're crying!"
<v David Rakoff>一个人希望自己能更像潘神，无拘无束地沉溺欲望，带着热情拥抱生活。另一个人想在关键时刻减少抽离感，能够更完整、更投入地感受到自己是一个人类，而不是那个旧科幻B级片里的桥段：“翠顿3000号面罩上的液体是什么？”“哦，太空机器人，你在哭泣！”

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:19.810
<v David Rakoff>We were going around the table, so the natural progression of things demanded that I eventually get a turn to weigh in as well. Suppose you're out to dinner with a group of triathletes, all discussing their training regimens. And you have no legs.
<v David Rakoff>我们围着桌子聊，所以事情的自然进展要求我也最终轮到发表意见。假设你和一群铁人三项运动员一起吃饭，都在讨论他们的训练方案。而你没有腿。

00:41:19.810 --> 00:41:33.460
<v David Rakoff>They can't flat-out ignore you, and they also can't say words to the effect of, "well, we all know what your event is. Getting all that marvelous wonderful parking, you lucky thing!"
<v David Rakoff>他们不能直接无视你，他们也不能说类似“好吧，我们都知道你的项目是什么。真是个幸运儿，能拿到那么多美妙极了的停车位！”之类的话。

00:41:33.460 --> 00:42:00.760
<v David Rakoff>It was uncomfortable, and I suspect more for me than for them. I have no idea. But thanks to my rapidly dividing cells, I no longer have that feeling-- although I remember it very well-- that if I just buckled down to the great work at hand, lived more authentically, stopped procrastinating, cut out sugar, then my best self was just there right around the corner.
<v David Rakoff>那种感觉很不舒服，我想对我比对他们更不舒服。我不知道。但多亏了我那迅速分裂的细胞，我不再有那种感觉了——尽管我记得很清楚——那种如果我只是勤奋地完成当下的伟大工作，活得更真实，停止拖延，不吃糖，那么我最好的自己就在拐角处的感觉。

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:07.060
<v David Rakoff>Yeah, no. I'm done with all that. I'm done with so many things.
<v David Rakoff>是的，不。我受够了所有那些。我受够了太多事情。

00:42:07.060 --> 00:42:15.800
<v David Rakoff>Like dancing. I've no idea if I can do it anymore. I've been, frankly, too frightened and too embarrassed to try it, even alone in my apartment.
<v David Rakoff>比如跳舞。我不知道我还能不能跳。老实说，我太害怕也太难为情了，连在公寓里独自尝试都不敢。

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:33.730
<v David Rakoff>There was a time however-- as recently as about a couple of years ago, when I was already one course of radiation and two surgeries into all this nonsense-- when doing this simple barre exercises while holding onto a kitchen chair achieved what they always used to do. What they're supposed to do.
<v David Rakoff>然而曾经有一段时间——就在大约几年前，当我已经在经历所有这些废话中的一轮放射治疗和两次手术时——当我抓着厨房的椅子练习简单的把杆练习时，它们实现了它们曾经一直做的事情。它们本该做的事情。

00:42:33.730 --> 00:43:15.490
<v David Rakoff>As best as I can describe it, it's the gestures themselves, their repetition, their slowness. It all hollows one out. One becomes a reed or a pipe, and the movement and the air pass through and you become this altered, humming, dare I say, beautiful working instrument of placement and form and concentration. But like I said, that's a long time ago. And a version of myself that has long since ceased to exist. Before I became such an observer-- I'm sorry.
<v David Rakoff>我能描述的最好方式是，这是姿势本身，它们的重复，它们的缓慢。这一切都掏空了一个人。你变成了一根芦苇或一根管子，动作和空气穿过你，你变成了一个变了样的、嗡嗡作响的，我敢说，美丽的、能够发挥作用的方位、形式和专注力的乐器。但就像我说的，那是很久以前的事了。是我那早已不复存在的版本。在我变成这样一个观察者之前——我很抱歉。

00:43:15.490 --> 00:44:37.730
<v Ira Glass>So at this point, David Rakoff walks away from the microphone. And just when it seems like he might walk off stage, like he quit, he turns, and turns again. And then raises his right knee, and then places that foot down again, and then traces a half circle on the ground with his left foot. And then he lunges, he arches his back, swings his right arm in an arc from low to high, all totally graceful. And then, he dances.
<v Ira Glass>就在这时，大卫·拉科夫走开了麦克风。正当他似乎要走下舞台时，像放弃了一样，他转过身，又转回来。然后抬起右膝，又把脚放下，用左脚在地上画了一个半圆。然后他冲刺，弓起背，右臂从低到高划出一道弧线，这一切都完全优雅。然后，他跳起舞来。

00:44:37.730 --> 00:45:02.340
<v David Rakoff>Look, mine is not a unique situation. Everybody loses ability-- everybody loses ability as they age. If you're lucky, this happens over the course of a few decades. If you're not--
<v David Rakoff>听着，我的情况并不独特。每个人都会失去能力——每个人随着年龄增长都会失去能力。如果你幸运，这会发生在几十年的时间里。如果你不幸运——

00:45:02.340 --> 00:45:50.650
<v David Rakoff>But the story is essentially the same. You go along the road as time and the elements lay waste to your luggage, scattering the contents into the bushes. Until there you are, standing with a battered and empty suitcase that frankly, no one wants to look at anymore. It's just the way it is. But how lovely those moments were, gone now except occasionally in dreams, when one could still turn to someone and promise them something truly worth their while, just by saying "hey, watch this."
<v David Rakoff>但故事本质上是一样的。你沿着路走，时间和大自然摧毁了你的行李，把里面的东西撒进灌木丛里。直到你站在那里，提着一个破旧而空空的行李箱，老实说，没有人想再看了。就是这样。但那些时刻是多么可爱，现在除了偶尔在梦中，当一个人还能转向某人并向他们承诺一些真正值得的东西时，只需说一句“嘿，看这个”，那些时刻就已经远去了。

00:45:50.650 --> 00:45:57.640
<v Ira Glass>David Rakoff is the author of many fine books, most recently Half Empty . This dance was choreographed by Monica Bill Barnes.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·拉科夫是许多优秀书籍的作者，最近的一本是《Half Empty》。这段舞蹈由莫妮卡·比尔·巴恩斯编舞。

00:45:57.640 --> 00:46:04.320
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Four: Turn Around, Bright Eyes.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了第四幕：转过身，明亮的眼睛。

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:10.330
<v Ira Glass>In his writing, David Sedaris can be kind of sharp tongued. But when you talk to him, when you meet him, he's really kind of a sweetheart.
<v Ira Glass>在大卫·塞达里斯的笔下，他可能有点尖酸刻薄。但当你和他交谈，当你见到他时，他其实是个甜心。

00:46:10.330 --> 00:46:26.970
<v Ira Glass>When my sister Karen met him at a reading, I remember she said to David, she said, "You're a lot nicer than I thought you would be, based on your books." And I remember David said, "Oh, I'm not nice. Just two-faced."
<v Ira Glass>我姐姐凯伦在一次朗读会上见到他时，我记得她对大卫说，她说：“你比我根据你的书想象的要好得多。”我记得大卫说：“噢，我不善良。只是两面派而已。”

00:46:26.970 --> 00:46:35.150
<v Ira Glass>So I'm pleased to say that tonight, you're really gonna see a story. You're gonna see David, how he really is tonight in real life, the real guy.
<v Ira Glass>所以我很高兴地说，今晚，你真的会看到一个故事。你真的会看到大卫，今晚在现实生活中他真实的样子，那个真实的人。

00:46:35.150 --> 00:46:45.830
<v Ira Glass>And the story that he's telling is about feelings that often go unexpressed, at least unexpressed at the moment they happen. Welcome David Sedaris, please.
<v Ira Glass>他讲述的故事是关于那些常常没能表达出来的感情，至少在发生的那一刻没表达出来。欢迎大卫·塞达里斯，请。

00:46:45.830 --> 00:47:00.830
<v Ira Glass>OK, an important fact about what the audience is seeing right here at this point in the show. David Sedaris takes the stage in full clown makeup. Red nose, white face, bald wig.
<v Ira Glass>好的，关于观众在节目中这一刻所看到的东西的一个重要事实。大卫·塞达里斯全妆打扮成小丑上台。红鼻子，白脸，秃头假发。

00:47:00.830 --> 00:47:18.300
<v David Sedaris>To those who don't travel very often, the Courtyard Marriott might seem like a decent enough hotel. It's clean, sure. And the staff is polite. I wouldn't give you $0.02 for its pillows though, and the tubs are far too shallow for my taste.
<v David Sedaris>对于那些不经常旅行的人来说，庭院万豪酒店可能看起来是一家还不错的酒店。当然，很干净。而且员工很有礼貌。不过我才不会花两分钱买他们的枕头，而且浴缸对我来说太浅了。

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:32.350
<v David Sedaris>In the deserted lobby of the one I stayed at not long ago in New Hampshire, there was a coffee bar. Not a Starbucks, but a place that "proudly served Starbucks" and sold it alongside breakfast cereals and prepackaged sandwiches.
<v David Sedaris>在我不久前住过的新罕布什尔州的那家酒店的空荡大堂里，有一个咖啡吧。不是星巴克，而是一个“自豪地供应星巴克”的地方，它和早餐麦片以及预包装三明治一起出售。

00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:56.750
<v David Sedaris>I noticed it on my way back from lunch, and just as I decided to get a cup of coffee, someone came around the corner and moved in ahead of me. I'd later learn that her name was Mrs. Dunstan, a towering, doe-colored, pyramid of a woman wearing oversized glasses and a short-sleeved linen blazer. Beside her was a man I guessed to be her husband. And after looking at the menu board, she turned to him.
<v David Sedaris>我在吃完午饭回来的路上注意到了它，就在我决定买杯咖啡时，有人绕过拐角走到了我前面。后来我才知道她的名字叫邓斯坦太太，一个高大的、母鹿色的、金字塔般的女性，戴着超大的眼镜，穿着短袖亚麻西装外套。她旁边是一个我猜是她丈夫的男人。在看了菜单牌后，她转向他。

00:47:56.750 --> 00:48:05.570
<v David Sedaris>"A latte," she said, "now is that the thing that Barbara likes to get? The one with whipped cream or is that called something else?"
<v David Sedaris>“拿铁，”她说，“那是芭芭拉喜欢喝的那种吗？带奶油的那个，还是叫别的什么名字？”

00:48:05.570 --> 00:48:10.490
<v David Sedaris>Oh [BEEP], I thought.
<v David Sedaris>噢[哔]，我想。

00:48:10.490 --> 00:48:26.830
<v David Sedaris>"I can do a latte with whipped cream on top", the young woman behind the counter said. She was fair and wore her shoulder-length hair pushed behind her ears. Tiny moles were scattered like buckshot across her face, which was bare but for a bit of eyeliner. "I can do one with flavors too."
<v David Sedaris>“我可以做一个加奶油的拿铁，”柜台后的年轻女性说。她皮肤白皙，肩长的头发别在耳后。小小的痣像霰弹一样散落在她的脸上，除了涂了一点眼线外，脸上没化什么妆。“我也可以加各种口味的。”

00:48:26.830 --> 00:48:31.040
<v David Sedaris>"Really?" Mrs. Dunstan said, "what sorts of flavors?"
<v David Sedaris>“真的吗？”邓斯坦太太说，“什么口味？”

00:48:31.040 --> 00:48:43.660
<v David Sedaris>In the end, she settled on caramel. Then her husband squinted up at the board, deciding after a good long while that he'd try one of those mocha something or others, and could he get that iced?
<v David Sedaris>最后，她选择了焦糖。然后她的丈夫眯着眼抬头看菜单牌，好一会儿才决定试一试那些摩卡什么什么的，而且能不能做成冰的？

00:48:43.660 --> 00:48:54.710
<v David Sedaris>As I groaned into my palm, he wandered off. His wife, meanwhile, leaned her bulk against the counter and began her genial interrogation. "Are you from this area?" she asked.
<v David Sedaris>当我对着手掌叹气时，他走开了。与此同时，他的妻子把那臃肿的身体靠在柜台上，开始了她和蔼可亲的盘问。“你是本地人吗？”她问。

00:48:54.710 --> 00:48:55.950
<v David Sedaris>"No, from Vermont."
<v David Sedaris>“不，从佛蒙特来的。”

00:48:55.950 --> 00:48:59.600
<v David Sedaris>"Well, that's interesting. What brought you here?"
<v David Sedaris>“哦，那很有意思。什么把你带到这里来了？”

00:48:59.600 --> 00:49:05.350
<v David Sedaris>I learned that the coffee person used to work at the town's other hotel, which had recently closed for remodeling.
<v David Sedaris>我得知那个咖啡吧的工作人员以前在镇上的另一家酒店工作，那家酒店最近为了装修关门了。

00:49:05.350 --> 00:49:16.740
<v David Sedaris>"So after it's done, will you stay put or go back over there?" Mrs. Dunstan asked. "Me, I have a son at the college, so that's what I'm doing. Just checking in. He's my second boy, actually. The first one went here too.
<v David Sedaris>“所以装修完之后，你会留在那里还是回那边去？”邓斯坦太太问。“我呢，我有一个儿子在大学，所以我就是做这个的。只是来看看。其实他是我第二个儿子。第一个也在这里上的学。

00:49:16.740 --> 00:49:39.280
<v David Sedaris>"He's not working in his field yet, but with unemployment as high as it is, he should be lucky to have anything at all. If I've told him that once, I've told him 100 times. But of course, being young, he's impatient, which is natural. Wants to set the world on fire and if it can't happen by tomorrow morning at 9:00 AM, then life's just unfair and hardly worth living. What about you, did you go to college?"
<v David Sedaris>“他还没在自己的领域工作，但现在失业率这么高，他能有份工作就该庆幸了。如果我对他说了那么一遍，我就对他说了100遍。但当然，年轻人嘛，没耐心，这很自然。想去大干一场，如果不能在明天早上9点前实现，那生活就不公平，甚至不值得活。那你呢，你上大学了吗？”

00:49:39.280 --> 00:49:56.100
<v David Sedaris>After what felt like weeks, the young woman finished with the orders. Two cups the size of waste paper baskets were placed upon the counter. And then Mr. Dunstan reappeared and pointed out the plate glass window toward a cluster of grim buildings on the other side of the parking lot.
<v David Sedaris>在感觉像过了几周后，那个年轻女性处理完了订单。柜台上放了两杯垃圾桶那么大的杯子。然后邓斯坦先生重新出现，指着落地窗外停车场另一边的一群阴森的建筑。

00:49:56.100 --> 00:49:58.580
<v David Sedaris>"What are those?" he asked.
<v David Sedaris>“那些是什么？”他问。

00:49:58.580 --> 00:50:04.210
<v David Sedaris>The young woman said they used to belong to the college. Of course, that was before they expanded the west side of the campus.
<v David Sedaris>那个年轻女性说那些以前属于大学。当然，那是在他们扩建校园西区之前。

00:50:04.210 --> 00:50:13.550
<v David Sedaris>"And when was that?" Mr. Dunstan asked. He was a good 10 years older than his wife, mid-70s maybe, and he wore a baseball cap with a tattered brim.
<v David Sedaris>“那是什么时候？”邓斯坦先生问。他比他妻子大整整10岁，可能七十岁出头，戴着一顶帽檐磨损的棒球帽。

00:50:13.550 --> 00:50:15.880
<v David Sedaris>"I beg your pardon?" the young woman said.
<v David Sedaris>“你说什么？”那个年轻女性说。

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:23.040
<v David Sedaris>"I said uh, when did they expand the west side of the campus? Was it recent, or did they do it a long time ago?"
<v David Sedaris>“我说呃，他们什么时候扩建校园西区的？是最近，还是很久以前？”

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:44.960
<v David Sedaris>Who the hell cares? I wanted to shout. What are you, the official historian of Who Gives a Damn College? Do you not notice that there's someone in line behind you? Someone who's been standing here, rocking back and forth on his heel for the last 10 minutes while you and that brontosaurus run your stupid mouths about nothing?
<v David Sedaris>谁他妈在乎啊？我想大喊。你是什么？“谁在乎大学”的官方历史学家吗？你没注意到你后面有人排队吗？一个人在这儿站了10分钟，脚后跟不停地前后摇晃，而你和那个腕龙在那儿喋喋不休地谈论着毫无意义的事情？

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:56.300
<v David Sedaris>I was this close to walking away, to marching off in a huff. But then Mrs. Dunstan would have turned to her husband and the girl behind the counter saying, "Some people!"
<v David Sedaris>我差一点就要走开了，气呼呼地走掉。但那样的话邓斯坦太太会转过身对她丈夫和柜台后的女孩说：“这些人啊！”

00:50:56.300 --> 00:51:22.990
<v David Sedaris>I'd gotten a similar reaction the previous morning when I'd squeezed past a couple standing side by side on the moving walkway connecting concourses A and B. "In a great big hurry to meet that heart attack!" the man called after me. I wanted to remind him that this was an airport, and that some of us had a tight connection, if that was OK.
<v David Sedaris>前一天早上我也遇到过类似的反应，当时我挤过了站在自动人行道上并排站着的一对夫妇。连接A和B航站楼的那种。“那么急着去见那场心脏病发作啊！”那个男人在我身后喊道。我想提醒他这里是机场，我们有些人要赶紧转机，如果没问题的话。

00:51:22.990 --> 00:51:36.700
<v David Sedaris>But of course, I had no connection. I just couldn't bear to see him and his wife standing side by side, blocking the way of someone who might have a tight connection.
<v David Sedaris>但当然，我没有转机。我只是无法忍受他和妻子并排站着，挡住了可能要赶转机的人的路。

00:51:36.700 --> 00:51:53.960
<v David Sedaris>The Dunstans' bill came to $8, which everyone agreed was a lot to pay for two cups of coffee. But they were large ones, and this was a vacation. Sort of.
<v David Sedaris>邓斯坦夫妇的账单是8美元，大家一致认为两杯咖啡花这么多钱确实很多。但它们是超大杯，而且这是度假。算是吧。

00:51:53.960 --> 00:52:07.000
<v David Sedaris>Not like a trip to Florida, but certainly couldn't do that at the drop of a hat. Especially with gas prices the way they are, and looking to go even higher.
<v David Sedaris>不像去佛罗里达的那种旅行，但肯定不能说走就走。尤其是油价现在这个样子，而且还要继续涨。

00:52:07.000 --> 00:52:27.260
<v David Sedaris>While talking, Mrs. Dunstan rummaged through her tremendous purse. Her wallet was eventually located, but then it seemed that the register was locked, so the best solution was to put the coffees on her bill. That's how I discovered her name. And her room number.
<v David Sedaris>说话间，邓斯坦太太翻遍了她巨大的钱包。钱包终于找到了，但收银机好像锁住了，所以最好的解决办法是把咖啡钱记在她的账上。我就是这样发现了她的名字。还有她的房间号。

00:52:27.260 --> 00:52:48.590
<v David Sedaris>My only question then was what time I should arrange her wake-up call for. Let's see how chatty you feel at 4:00 AM, I thought. Then it was all about returning the wallet to the purse and getting that all safely zipped up before picking her drink off the counter and starting in on her long goodbye.
<v David Sedaris>我当时唯一的问题是我该给她的叫醒电话定在几点。我想看看你在凌晨4点还会不会觉得那么爱聊天，我想。然后一切就变成了把钱包放回手袋，在从柜台上拿起她的饮料并开始她漫长的告别之前把一切拉链拉好。

00:52:48.590 --> 00:53:14.490
<v David Sedaris>When the two of them finally lumbered off toward the elevator, I approached the counter, hoping the woman behind it would roll her eyes, acknowledging that something really needed to be done about people like the Dunstans. She didn't, though. So I decided I would hate her as much as I had hated them. When she told me that her little stand didn't serve regular brew coffee, I hated her even more.
<v David Sedaris>当他们两个终于摇摇晃晃地走向电梯时，我走近柜台，希望那个柜台后的女人能翻个白眼，承认真的得对邓斯坦夫妇这样的人做点什么。但她没有。所以我决定要像讨厌他们一样讨厌她。当她告诉我她的小摊位不供应普通热咖啡时，我更讨厌她了。

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:23.050
<v David Sedaris>"I can do you a nice cappuccino," she said. "Or an iced latte, maybe." This last word was delivered to my back as I stormed out the door.
<v David Sedaris>“我可以给你做杯不错的卡布奇诺，”她说。“或者冰拿铁，也许。”当我想暴怒地冲出门口时，最后这个词是对着我的后背说的。

00:53:23.050 --> 00:53:47.870
<v David Sedaris>Then it was up the street and around the corner to a real coffee place. The pierced and tattooed staff members scowled at my approach, and I placed my order confident that they would hate the Dunstans as much, or possibly even more, than they already hated me.
<v David Sedaris>然后我走上街，绕过拐角去了一家真正的咖啡店。穿刺纹身的工作人员在我走近时皱了皱眉头，我下达了订单，相信他们会像讨厌我一样，或者可能比讨厌我更讨厌邓斯坦夫妇。

00:53:47.870 --> 00:53:58.810
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris. He's the author, most recently, of Squirrel Seeks Chipmunk: a Modest Bestiary. Again, OK Go.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯。他是作家，最近的一本书是《Squirrel Seeks Chipmunk: a Modest Bestiary》。再次重申，OK Go。

00:53:58.810 --> 00:55:02.650
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC--"DO WHAT YOU WANT" BY  OK GO]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐--“DO WHAT YOU WANT” BY  OK GO]

00:55:02.650 --> 00:55:11.500
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Seth Lind and myself. Our live cinema event was directed by David Stern. Annette Jolles was the associate producer.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目今天由塞斯·林德和我本人制作。我们的现场影院活动由大卫·斯特恩执导。安妮特·乔尔斯担任副制片人。

00:55:11.500 --> 00:55:15.200
<v Ira Glass>The entire crew here are incredible. They're like NASA scientists.
<v Ira Glass>这里的整个团队都很不可思议。他们就像NASA的科学家一样。

00:55:15.200 --> 00:55:24.800
<v Ira Glass>The executive producers for cinema are Robert and Julie Borchard-Young. Lenny Laxer was the technical manager. Emily Condon was the associate producer for today's show. Miki Meek helped produce the show.
<v Ira Glass>电影的执行制片人是罗伯特·博查德-杨和朱莉·博查德-杨。兰尼·拉瑟担任技术经理。艾米丽·康顿担任今天节目的副制片人。米基·米克协助制作了节目。

00:55:24.800 --> 00:55:38.160
<v Ira Glass>Today's show was recorded live at NYU's Skirball Center for the Performing Arts. Amy Coombs, technical director. Jason Adams, production manager.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目是在纽约大学斯基尔鲍尔表演艺术中心现场录制的。艾米·库姆斯，技术总监。杰森·亚当斯，制作经理。

00:55:38.160 --> 00:55:48.360
<v Ira Glass>Claire Keane coordinated all the illustrations and animations. She did the poster art, just incredible. Station outreach by Sean Nesbitt, Kathy Twist, Roger Gamal and Heidi Schultz.
<v Ira Glass>克莱尔·基恩协调了所有的插图和动画。她做了海报艺术，简直太棒了。肖恩·内斯比特、凯西·特威斯特、罗杰·加马尔和海蒂·舒尔茨负责电台联络。

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:54.020
<v Ira Glass>Thanks to the many, many public radio stations who participated and brought people out. Thanks to my wife Anaheed.
<v Ira Glass>感谢参与并带来观众的许多、许多公共广播电台。感谢我的妻子阿娜希德。

00:55:54.020 --> 00:56:06.650
<v Ira Glass>Our staff, the greatest radio production staff ever. Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Sarah Koenig, Jonathan Menjivar, Lisa Pollak, Brian Reed, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer, Julie Snyder.
<v Ira Glass>我们的团队，有史以来最棒的广播制作团队。亚历克斯·布隆伯格、本·卡尔霍恩、莎拉·柯尼格、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、丽莎·波拉克、布莱恩·里德、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克。我们的高级制片人，朱莉·斯奈德。

00:56:06.650 --> 00:56:12.150
<v Ira Glass>Production help, yeah, from Matt Kielty and Elna Baker. Adrianne Mathiowetz runs our website.
<v Ira Glass>是的，制作帮助来自马特·凯尔蒂和埃尔娜·贝克。阿德里安娜·马蒂奥维茨负责我们的网站。

00:56:12.150 --> 00:56:21.900
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International, WBEZ management oversight for our program by our boss, Mr. Torey Malatia.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行，我们老板托里·马拉蒂亚先生对我们的节目进行管理监督。

00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:32.360
<v Ira Glass>You know, how did I end up in this job? How did it happen? He came to me over and over, walked up to me in restaurants and on the street saying the same thing over and over again.
<v Ira Glass>你知道，我是怎么走上这份工作的？是怎么发生的？他一遍又一遍地来找我，在餐馆里和街上走到我面前，一遍又一遍地重复同样的话。

00:56:32.360 --> 00:56:44.050
<v Tig Notaro>Excuse me, sorry to bother you. But I just have to tell you, I love your voice.
<v Tig Notaro>不好意思，抱歉打扰了。但我必须告诉你，我喜欢你的声音。

00:56:44.050 --> 00:57:42.740
<v Ira Glass>You mean my speaking voice? I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>你是说我的说话声？我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。

00:57:42.740 --> 00:57:47.740
<v Announcer>PRI. Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI。国际公共广播电台。
